of those signature strings in the order of first appearance? Or alphabetical? But the schema doesn't specify order.\n\nBut note: capacity is given, so there is a bound. If capacity is 3, maybe we only keep memory for the last 3 unique signatures? That seems plausible: a cache of recent unique signatures with their last trial info. The \"capacity\" could be the maximum number of entries in memory. So we maintain a dictionary for up to capacity entries. When a new trial comes, we update the entry for that signature (if present) or add if not and memory full, evict the least recently used? Or the oldest? Typically LRU or FIFO.\n\nBut the trials have reflections that may be new each time. So each trial has a signature and reflection. The memory entry for a signature should reflect its last trial (with reflection and success). The \"last_trial\" would be the index of that trial. This seems natural: maintain a memory of the most recent occurrence of each signature, up to capacity.\n\nThen the signatures array would be the list of signatures present in memory (maybe in the order they appear in the memory array? The memory array order could be the order of last trial descending? Or insertion order? The schema doesn't specify order of memory items. The output must be JSON, but order of array items is not specified except that it must be valid JSON. However, the test harness may check exact element order, because JSON arrays are ordered. So we need to infer the ordering rule.\n\nLet's try to simulate one case with assumption: maintain dict mapping signature to (last_trial, reflection, success). For each trial in order (index i starting from 1 or 0?), update. After processing all trials, memory = list of dicts for each signature in dict (maybe ordered by last_trial descending? Or by order of first occurrence?). Then signatures = list of signatures in same order as memory.\n\nBut we have capacity. So we need to enforce that the dict never has more than capacity entries. When ad